  "question": "Apply the method of differentials to estimate $(15.9)^{\\frac{1}{4}}$.",
  "answeroption": "D",
  "explanation": "<b>Step 1:</b> Let $y=x^{\\frac{1}{4}}$. Choose $x=16$ and $dx=-0.1$, since $15.9=16-0.1$.<br><b>Step 2:</b> Differentiating, $$dy=\\frac{1}{4x^{\\frac{3}{4}}}dx$$<br><b>Step 3:</b> Since $16^{\\frac{3}{4}}=8$, $$dy=\\frac{-0.1}{4(8)}=-0.003125$$<br><b>Step 4:</b> Also, $16^{\\frac{1}{4}}=2$. Therefore, $$(15.9)^{\\frac{1}{4}}\\approx2-0.003125$$<br><b>Step 5:</b> Hence, $(15.9)^{\\frac{1}{4}}\\approx1.996875$.",
